README-KEEP -- taking things off this collection================================================This collection is meant to be RUN IN PLACE. Mount it as /h0, put its CMDSdirectories on your PATH, and everything on it works where it lies. Nothingneeds installing.When a program earns its place, `keep' copies it onto your own disk.    keep <name>...      the program, and whatever it needs    keep -n <name>...   dry run: say what would be written, touch nothing    keep -f <name>...   overwrite files already there (otherwise refused)    kept                what has been taken, and how much it came to    drop <name>...      put back exactly what keep wrote    drop -n <name>...   dry runBy default it copies FROM /h0 TO /dd. Set KEEP_FROM and KEEP_TO to sayotherwise:    export KEEP_TO=/h1    keep hack cookie lessWhat `keep' takes with it--------------------------Not just the binary. DOC/DEPENDS lists, for every program, each absolute pathits code contains -- generated by scanning the binaries, not by guesswork --and keep brings any of those that exist here. `keep cookie' takes cookie, itssayings and its sayhash. `keep hack' takes hack and the termcap it wants.A dependency that is a DIRECTORY is skipped and said so out loud: hack's/h0/games/hack/playground is a directory of bones files and scores, andcopying it is your decision, not ours.A file named twice is taken once. A program that wants both /dd/sys/termcapand /h0/sys/termcap is asking for one file.Nothing is overwritten. If the destination already has a file of that name,keep refuses, names it, and moves on -- your own `ls' is never replaced byours. `-f' overrides that.Why `drop' is safe------------------Every file keep writes is recorded in SYS/kept: the program it came for, whereit went, its size, and its MD5.drop reads that receipt and, for each file:    gone already        says so, moves on    checksum matches    removes it    CHECKSUM CHANGED    KEEPS IT, and tells youSo a file you have written to is, by definition, no longer the file keep putthere -- and drop will not touch it. Your saves, your scores, your editedconfiguration survive `drop', by construction rather than by our beingcareful. Demonstrated:    $ keep cookie    kept /h1/CMDS/cookie    kept /h1/GAMES/cookie/sayhash    kept /h1/GAMES/cookie/sayings    $ echo "one of my own" >> /h1/GAMES/cookie/sayings    $ drop cookie    dropped /h1/CMDS/cookie    dropped /h1/GAMES/cookie/sayhash    drop: /h1/GAMES/cookie/sayings has CHANGED since it was kept -- left aloneHow they are written--------------------These three are bash scripts, and their source is in SRC/keep. Run them as`bash /dd/CMDS/keep ...' -- OS-9 forks modules, not text files, so a scriptis not directly executable the way it would be on Unix.They are written around four things this bash and this OS-9 do differently,each of which cost a debugging round:  * command substitution does not inherit PATH and cannot see shell    functions, so there is none: output goes to a file and `read' picks it up  * the -f and -d tests report false for files on a host-directory mount, and    are case-sensitive where OS-9's own file opens are not -- `ls path' and    its exit status is the reliable test  * there is no /dev/null. The bit bucket is /nil  * mkdir has no -p, so each directory component is made in turn